To configure Azure AD B2C custom policies to handle sign-ins using the identities property (e.g., issuerAssignedId and signInType), follow these step-by-step instructions:


---

Step 1: Prerequisites

1. Azure AD B2C Tenant:

Ensure you have an Azure AD B2C tenant configured.

Set up Identity Experience Framework in Azure AD B2C.



2. Download the Starter Pack:

Download the Azure AD B2C custom policy starter pack.



3. Prepare Tenant-Specific Values:

Replace placeholder values in the starter pack with your tenant details:

yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com

B2C policy name prefix (e.g., B2C_1A_).






---

Step 2: Define Claim Types in TrustFrameworkBase.xml

Ensure the necessary claims for sign-in are defined.

1. Open the TrustFrameworkBase.xml file.


2. Add or ensure the following claim definitions exist for signInName and issuerAssignedId:

<ClaimType Id="signInName">
  <DisplayName>Sign In Name</DisplayName>
  <DataType>string</DataType>
  <DefaultPartnerClaimTypes>
    <Protocol Name="OAuth2" PartnerClaimType="username" />
  </DefaultPartnerClaimTypes>
  <UserHelpText>The sign-in name used for logging in</UserHelpText>
</ClaimType>

<ClaimType Id="issuerAssignedId">
  <DisplayName>Issuer Assigned ID</DisplayName>
  <DataType>string</DataType>
  <UserHelpText>The unique ID assigned by the issuer</UserHelpText>
</ClaimType>




---

Step 3: Update TrustFrameworkExtensions.xml

3.1 Modify the Sign-In Technical Profile

1. Open TrustFrameworkExtensions.xml.


2. Locate the LocalAccountSignIn or LocalAccountSignInUsingEmail technical profile.


3. Update the technical profile to validate sign-ins using the identities property.

Example:

<TechnicalProfile Id="LocalAccountSignInUsingEmail">
  <DisplayName>Local Account SignIn</DisplayName>
  <Protocol Name="Proprietary" Handler="Web.TPEngine.Providers.LocalAccountProvider">
    <Metadata>
      <Item Key="Operation">Read</Item>
      <Item Key="RaiseErrorIfClaimsPrincipalDoesNotExist">true</Item>
    </Metadata>
    <InputClaims>
      <!-- Use issuerAssignedId for sign-in -->
      <InputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="signInName" PartnerClaimType="identities[issuerAssignedId]" Required="true" />
    </InputClaims>
    <OutputClaims>
      <OutputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="objectId" />
      <OutputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="authenticationSource" />
    </OutputClaims>
    <ValidationTechnicalProfiles>
      <ValidationTechnicalProfile ReferenceId="login-NonInteractive" />
    </ValidationTechnicalProfiles>
  </Protocol>
</TechnicalProfile>



3.2 Update Validation Technical Profile

Ensure the login-NonInteractive technical profile is updated to handle issuerAssignedId and signInType for validation.

Example:

<TechnicalProfile Id="login-NonInteractive">
  <Metadata>
    <Item Key="UserMessageIfClaimsPrincipalDoesNotExist">An account could not be found for the provided sign-in identifier.</Item>
    <Item Key="UserMessageIfInvalidPassword">Your password is incorrect.</Item>
  </Metadata>
  <InputClaims>
    <InputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="signInName" PartnerClaimType="identities[issuerAssignedId]" Required="true" />
    <InputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="password" Required="true" />
  </InputClaims>
  <OutputClaims>
    <OutputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="objectId" />
    <OutputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="displayName" />
  </OutputClaims>
</TechnicalProfile>


---

Step 4: Update the User Journey

1. In TrustFrameworkExtensions.xml, locate the SignUpOrSignIn user journey.


2. Ensure the signInName claim is used during the login step.

Example:

<OrchestrationStep Order="1" Type="CombinedSignInAndSignUp" ContentDefinitionReferenceId="api.signuporsignin">
  <ClaimsExchange>
    <ClaimsExchangeId>LocalAccountSigninExchange</ClaimsExchangeId>
    <TechnicalProfileReferenceId>LocalAccountSignInUsingEmail</TechnicalProfileReferenceId>
  </ClaimsExchange>
</OrchestrationStep>




---

Step 5: Upload and Test Policies

5.1 Upload Policies

1. Go to Azure AD B2C > Identity Experience Framework > Upload custom policy.


2. Upload the files in this order:

TrustFrameworkBase.xml

TrustFrameworkExtensions.xml

Other policy files (e.g., SignUpOrSignIn.xml).




5.2 Test Sign-In

1. Navigate to the Run User Flow section.


2. Test the custom policy by trying to sign in with a user whose identity has the identities property.




---

Step 6: Verify User Data

Ensure users in your B2C tenant have the identities property configured. Use Microsoft Graph API to check.

Example: Retrieve User Data

GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/users?$filter=identities/any(c:c/issuerAssignedId eq 'user@example.com' and c/issuer eq 'yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com')

Example User JSON

{
  "identities": [
    {
      "signInType": "emailAddress",
      "issuer": "yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com",
      "issuerAssignedId": "user@example.com"
    }
  ],
  "displayName": "John Doe",
  "accountEnabled": true
}


---

Key Notes

1. Use identities Property:

issuerAssignedId: User's sign-in identifier (e.g., email or username).

signInType: Type of sign-in (e.g., emailAddress, userName, etc.).



2. Ensure Proper Claim Mapping:

Map issuerAssignedId to signInName for seamless integration in your policy.



3. Test Thoroughly:

Verify that users can sign in using their issuerAssignedId with the specified signInType.





